    <record name="IconSet"
            c:type="GtkIconSet"
            opaque="1"
            glib:type-name="GtkIconSet"
            glib:get-type="gtk_icon_set_get_type"
            c:symbol-prefix="icon_set">
      <source-position filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkstyle.h" line="58"/>
      <constructor name="new" c:identifier="gtk_icon_set_new">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.c"
             line="1115">Creates a new #GtkIconSet. A #GtkIconSet represents a single icon
in various sizes and widget states. It can provide a #GdkPixbuf
for a given size and state on request, and automatically caches
some of the rendered #GdkPixbuf objects.

Normally you would use gtk_widget_render_icon() instead of
using #GtkIconSet directly. The one case where you'd use
#GtkIconSet is to create application-specific icon sets to place in
a #GtkIconFactory.</doc>
        <source-position filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.h"
                         line="120"/>
        <return-value transfer-ownership="full">
          <doc xml:space="preserve"
               filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.c"
               line="1128">a new #GtkIconSet</doc>
          <type name="IconSet" c:type="GtkIconSet*"/>
        </return-value>
      </constructor>
      <constructor name="new_from_pixbuf"
                   c:identifier="gtk_icon_set_new_from_pixbuf">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.c"
             line="1146">Creates a new #GtkIconSet with @pixbuf as the default/fallback
source image. If you don't add any additional #GtkIconSource to the
icon set, all variants of the icon will be created from @pixbuf,
using scaling, pixelation, etc. as required to adjust the icon size
or make the icon look insensitive/prelighted.</doc>
        <source-position filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.h"
                         line="121"/>
        <return-value transfer-ownership="full">
          <doc xml:space="preserve"
               filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.c"
               line="1156">a new #GtkIconSet</doc>
          <type name="IconSet" c:type="GtkIconSet*"/>
        </return-value>
        <parameters>
          <parameter name="pixbuf" transfer-ownership="none">
            <doc xml:space="preserve"
                 filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.c"
                 line="1148">a #GdkPixbuf</doc>
            <type name="GdkPixbuf.Pixbuf" c:type="GdkPixbuf*"/>
          </parameter>
        </parameters>
      </constructor>
      <method name="add_source" c:identifier="gtk_icon_set_add_source">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.c"
             line="1696">Icon sets have a list of #GtkIconSource, which they use as base
icons for rendering icons in different states and sizes. Icons are
scaled, made to look insensitive, etc. in
gtk_icon_set_render_icon(), but #GtkIconSet needs base images to
work with. The base images and when to use them are described by
a #GtkIconSource.

This function copies @source, so you can reuse the same source immediately
without affecting the icon set.

An example of when you'd use this function: a web browser's "Back
to Previous Page" icon might point in a different direction in
Hebrew and in English; it might look different when insensitive;
and it might change size depending on toolbar mode (small/large
icons). So a single icon set would contain all those variants of
the icon, and you might add a separate source for each one.

You should nearly always add a "default" icon source with all
fields wildcarded, which will be used as a fallback if no more
specific source matches. #GtkIconSet always prefers more specific
icon sources to more generic icon sources. The order in which you
add the sources to the icon set does not matter.

gtk_icon_set_new_from_pixbuf() creates a new icon set with a
default icon source based on the given pixbuf.</doc>
        <source-position filename="gtk+-2.24.33/gtk/gtkiconfactory.h"
                         line="139"/>
        <return-value transfer-ownership="none">
          <type name="none" c:type="void"/>
        </return-value>
        <parameters>
          <instance-parameter name="icon_set" transfer-ownership="none">
            <doc xml:space="preserve"
